     <section anchor="conclusions" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-12">
       <name slugifiedName="name-conclusions">Conclusions</name>
-      <t indent="0" pn="section-12-1">Given the current effort to implement RED, we believe this is the
-right time for router vendors to examine how to implement congestion
-avoidance mechanisms that do not depend on packet drops alone.  With
-the increased deployment of applications and transports sensitive to
-the delay and loss of a single packet (e.g., realtime traffic, short
-web transfers), depending on packet loss as a normal congestion
-notification mechanism appears to be insufficient (or at the very
-least, non-optimal).</t>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-12-1">Given the current effort to implement RED, we believe this is the right time for router vendors to examine how to implement congestion avoidance mechanisms that do not depend on packet drops alone.  With the increased deployment of applications and transports sensitive to the delay and loss of a single packet (e.g., realtime traffic, short web transfers), depending on packet loss as a normal congestion notification mechanism appears to be insufficient (or at the very least, non-optimal).</t>
     </section>
